# @wpt/web runtime env vars
#
# ── The one switch: which backend the BFF talks to ───────────────────────────
# API_BASE_URL is the ONLY variable that decides mock vs real. Point it at a
# backend and linking, sessions, read receipts and typing all become real; leave
# it blank and the app still renders, but linking reports a retryable "couldn't
# reach the linking service". No screen branches on which one is in use.
API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000

# Optional. The browser dials the realtime push channel itself (it holds a
# single-scope link ticket, which authorises exactly one link room and nothing
# else), so it needs an origin IT can reach. Set this only when that origin
# differs from API_BASE_URL — e.g. API_BASE_URL is an internal docker name for
# the BFF while the phone/browsers reach the host on the LAN. When it is absent
# the browser is handed API_BASE_URL, so a single variable is genuinely enough.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.20:4000

# ── Development sample chat data (WEB-2.1 → WEB-2.5) ─────────────────────────
# No configuration is needed to use it: `next dev` automatically loads eight
# fictional conversations (five direct, three group, one 40+ message thread) so
# the chat list, unread badges, search, grouping and media states can all be
# exercised without a chat backend. The sample data lives entirely in
# app/lib/dev-fixtures/ and is reachable only through app/lib/chat-source.ts.
#
# It CANNOT appear in a production build. app/lib/chat-fixtures-gate.ts fails
# closed — the fixtures require NODE_ENV to be exactly "development" or "test",
# and app/lib/chat-source.ts reads process.env.NODE_ENV directly so the bundler
# drops the import from a production build entirely.
#
# Set this to 0 if you are developing against the real chat relay and do not want
# sample conversations: NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAT_FIXTURES=0
#
# A linked session is still required to see the chat UI (linking is not bypassed
# by sample data) — use the development login QR below to obtain one.

# ── Chat relay (apps/web/server/wpt-server.mjs) ──────────────────────────────
# The chat mock. It is CHAT ONLY: it no longer mints, scans, confirms or pushes
# link codes, and it has no QR page. Web linking has one implementation, in
# apps/backend.
PORT=3000

# Development-only test login. Not present in a production build at all.
#
# It signs in whichever REAL account the backend holds for the allowlisted phone
# number — the number is sent, the backend resolves it against its `users` table.
# No account identity is hard-coded anywhere in the Web app; see the note below
# the NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_QR_ORIGIN example for how to create that account.
#
# The dev server listens on 0.0.0.0 (see the `dev` script) so a phone on the same
# LAN can reach it. NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_QR_ORIGIN is the address the development QR
# is built from. Leave it unset and the page's own origin is used, which is
# already right when you opened the page by its LAN address. Set it when you
# opened the page on localhost, because a phone resolves "localhost" to ITSELF
# and the scan would silently fail.
#
# Never commit a specific address; use your machine's own:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_QR_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.10:3000
#
# The QR it produces is http://<that address>/dev/test-login?phone=9876543210,
# which any ordinary QR scanner can open. That route is refused with 404 in a
# production build, only ever signs in the allowlisted test PHONE, and carries no
# token, OTP, PIN, key or session credential. The session still comes from the
# backend's real redeem and still lands only in an HttpOnly cookie.
#
# WHICH ACCOUNT that phone signs in as is decided by the backend, not here: the
# route sends the number and apps/backend resolves it against its real `users`
# store (users.phone_number -> id + name, the same lookup OTP verify does). There
# is no hard-coded account and no fallback. So the number must already exist as a
# real account in your database - register it once through the normal onboarding
# OTP flow (apps/backend/src/db/test-existing-account-flow.ts drives that against a
# running backend) - otherwise the route reports "No account exists for ...".
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_QR_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.10:3000

# REQUIRED for the chat relay's WebSocket: it verifies the 60s realtime ticket
# that apps/backend mints at POST /api/session/ticket. It MUST be byte-identical
# to the backend's JWT_SECRET, otherwise every `join` is refused with 4001 and
# the client can open the socket but will never receive live events.
JWT_SECRET=

# The relay base the same-origin /api/relay BFF proxy forwards guarded chat
# traffic to. Server-only; the browser never targets it directly. Defaults to
# http://localhost:3000.
# WPT_RELAY_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional. The CHAT socket is a raw WebSocket to the relay, not the link
# channel: when this is unset the browser dials its own origin (same host/port
# as the page), which is the normal dev and single-host setup. Set it only when
# the relay is on a different origin the browser can reach.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WPT_API_URL=http://192.168.1.20:3000
